Definition:
    -Singleton pattern is a pattern where a class can have only one instance,
     and have a global point of access to that instance
    -so basically the class can have only one object.

How to do it?
    -well, we have to use the concept of private constructors and static methods
    -if a class has private constructor then no other class can instantiate it.
    -but there is a way around it, we can use an if condition with the static keyword
     to make sure only one object can be created.

Why singleton pattern?
    -well for logging we have logger object, for example in spring boot framework we have
     sl4j logger, which behaves like a singleton object because we don't want multiple
     logger objects writing different things at the same time it would be chaos
    -cache manager
    -Database connection pool also requires a single connection object not multiple
    -Normally modern frameworks do the job as they handle the object lifecycle

Multithreading concern:
    -now see multiple threads can run at the same time and then 2 or mor threads can call the getInstance method
     at the same time and now guess what, 2 instances of the classes have been created because both of them at the exact
     time saw that initially no object was created and hence both of them created it.
    -This causes an issue, we tried to implement simpleton pattern but multithreading issue is breaking it
    -So the solution is we can us the  `synchronised` keyword on the getInstance method so that no 2 threads can call it at
     the same time, the threads have to wait.
    -But it introduces another problem and that is synchronisation overhead, we need to have synchronisation only when the object
     has to be created the first time, once its created we don't need to synchronise anymore.
    -Solution 1:
        we can keep the keep the synchronised keyword as it is as if the code isnt that critical (but remember
        synchronised keyword lowers performance by a factor of 100)
    -Solution 2:
        we can use an Eager method of creating the object rather than lazy method
    -Solution 3:
        if the instance exists, return that instance, if not only then use the synchronised keyword
        this is called double check locking
    -Solution 4:
        This is the most robust and clean way of implementation, use enums, enums can have methods and fields